4
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:19,189
[Music]

5
00:00:19,199 --> 00:00:23,590
Hello all, welcome to this episode of soft and engineering radio.

6
00:00:23,590 --> 00:00:29,349
Our guests today are Connor Goodrum and Jacob Bisawadi.

7
00:00:29,349 --> 00:00:33,350
Connor is a senior data scientist and Jacob is senior engineering manager at Deepgram.

8
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:45,990
Deepgram is a foundational AI company specializing in voice technology and enabling advanced voice applications across many businesses sectors including healthcare and customer service.

9
00:00:45,990 --> 00:00:51,110
DRAM solutions include conversational AI agents.

10
00:00:51,110 --> 00:00:53,189
Welcome to this show Connor and Jacob.

11
00:00:53,189 --> 00:00:57,511
Before we get started, is there anything you'd like to add to your bio Connor? No, that about sums it up.

12
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:00,790
Thanks very much for having me. Excited to talk today, Jacob.

13
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:02,870
No, thank you. Likewise.

14
00:01:02,870 --> 00:01:06,230
Very excited to be here. Glad I've got my man Connor right alongside me.

15
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:08,310
Thank you.

16
00:01:08,310 --> 00:01:17,910
So, our topic and our focus today is testing ML models for enterprise use cases, enterprise products.

17
00:01:17,910 --> 00:01:26,630
Just to set context, could you explain the relationship between a data science model, an ML model, and an LLM?

18
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:35,590
Well, I would say that everybody's got their own vernacular about how all these things fit together, largely the way that I consider them.

19
00:01:35,590 --> 00:01:38,390
An LLM is just one type of ML model.

20
00:01:38,390 --> 00:01:47,670
And similarly, we use data science approaches to train various types of models, one of which could be an LLM, but they all have their sort of specific use cases and applications.

21
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:57,510
Yeah, maybe just to build on that, Connor, when we think about the field of data science, I guess I could say traditionally, even though it's a relatively new discipline,

22
00:01:57,510 --> 00:02:04,630
I think we see a lot of initial applications that maybe grew almost out of the big data movement.

23
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:07,670
That was the key buzzword but 10, 15, 20 years ago, right?

24
00:02:07,670 --> 00:02:19,589
And we see things like teams of analysts inside a larger enterprise that are developing models maybe to forecast revenue growth across market segments.

25
00:02:19,589 --> 00:02:27,190
And we have generally well ststructured inputs applied to a narrow range of questions and mostly for an internal audience.

26
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:29,750
And of course, there's a lot of people doing great work there.

27
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:33,910
And I don't mean to oversimplify how complex that kind of work can be.

28
00:02:33,910 --> 00:02:35,750
It's it's extremely hard stuff.

29
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:39,670
And forecasting revenues is pretty darn important for any company to get right.

30
00:02:39,670 --> 00:02:48,390
And I think what's really interesting now and what I think provokes this kind of conversation is now we see the intense productization of those techniques at a greater scale,

31
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:56,390
especially in so far as they more and more approximate human intelligence and therefore are justifiably called AI.

32
00:02:56,390 --> 00:03:00,070
So when we think about machine learning models in this context,

33
00:03:00,070 --> 00:03:11,030
we're thinking about things like accepting unstructured data and the model is no longer a limited set of results that are going to be curated and delivered in human time to a known audience,

34
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:20,550
but it's going to be delivered in real time to wide audiences with consumer focuses without any human in the loop checking on those results in the meantime,

35
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:23,910
which of course exposes a whole host of concerns on the quality front.

36
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:25,750
Thanks for that, Jacob.

37
00:03:25,750 --> 00:03:28,309
So I think that leads me to my next question.

38
00:03:28,309 --> 00:03:39,030
Given this expanded focus, is that what leads companies to think of themselves as an AI first company or a foundational AI company?

39
00:03:39,030 --> 00:03:41,030
And what is the relation between these two terms?

40
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:52,949
I think justifiably AI first companies are those whose product really revolves around delivering value to their end customer through some kind of AI tooling.

41
00:03:52,949 --> 00:03:58,789
I think that the really useful designation or distinction that you brought up there is foundational versus not.

42
00:03:58,789 --> 00:04:05,750
So there are a lot of AI first companies that are delivering really cool products that are built on top of other more foundational technologies.

43
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:18,949
And the difference between some of those companies that are doing really neat things and a company like Deepgram Graham or other big players in the space like OpenAI and Enthropic is we're developing new models from scratch.

44
00:04:18,959 --> 00:04:30,790
maybe influenced by what's going on across the industry, informed by the latest developments in the research world, the academic world, but we're essentially developing new things from scratch,

45
00:04:30,790 --> 00:04:36,150
empowering other people to build all sorts of applications on top of almost infrastructural AI pieces.

46
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:49,110
The kind of testing that a foundational AI company has to do is also different from what potentially an AI first company that uses AI infrastructure would do.

47
00:04:49,110 --> 00:04:56,070
and it would probably build upon the testing that a foundational AI company has in place. Is that a fair summarization?

48
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:59,189
Absolutely.

49
00:04:59,189 --> 00:05:05,749
I would say in building upon other people's models, it's easy to sort of point the finger when something goes wrong and be able to say like,

50
00:05:05,749 --> 00:05:14,070
oh well, we're using this provider's model to do this part of our software stack and therefore we can really only test inputs and outputs.

51
00:05:14,070 --> 00:05:26,150
Being on the foundational side, we really have the control to be able to go in and tweak parameters or adjust the model itself in an attempt to design them out rather than working around them.

52
00:05:26,150 --> 00:05:28,469
And that's a huge huge advantage.

53
00:05:28,469 --> 00:05:34,390
Yeah, I think a neat pattern that we've seen emerge is our customers are often times AI first companies.

54
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:43,670
They're building upon deepgram as one of their foundational AI infrastructural pieces, but these are still AI companies offering AI enabled products.

55
00:05:43,670 --> 00:05:49,990
And so the kinds of testing that they do of our system is a certain kind of testing in the AI world.

56
00:05:49,990 --> 00:05:54,310
One of the most common ways we see this when we're talking with potential customers is is the bake off.

57
00:05:54,320 --> 00:06:06,629
the classic bakeoff that's been in practice for decades across so many industries where they take their actual production audio that they might want to turn into text and run it through different providers.

58
00:06:06,629 --> 00:06:12,230
And despite what you may read about these different providers and whatever benchmarks they've previously published,

59
00:06:12,230 --> 00:06:20,870
really the thing that matters most for these customers is how do the different options you're comparing do on your production audio,

60
00:06:20,870 --> 00:06:25,510
not on the benchmark set that you read a paper on, but how does it work for your customers?

61
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:35,830
This is actually a place where we try to really thrive because we play hard and compete hard in the customization space and try to work with our customers in a really hightouch way in that manner.

62
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:39,590
But that's a serious form of testing that has a lot of nuance.

63
00:06:39,590 --> 00:06:45,350
And then of course on the deepgram side we're thinking about that generalized across our entire customer base.

64
00:06:45,350 --> 00:06:52,390
All the different market segments that we're addressing, all the different domains that we seek to represent well in our modeling.

65
00:06:52,390 --> 00:06:55,990
So how exactly is testing ML models different from traditional software testing?

66
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:07,749
Well, I can say from the sort of data science side of the house, testing ML models for enterprise, enterprises tend to be far more riskaverse than your normal user.

67
00:07:07,749 --> 00:07:10,550
Enterprises have established software stacks.

68
00:07:10,550 --> 00:07:20,629
They generally have much stricter requirements around latency and accuracy, uptime, all of which need to be met in order for them to be able to serve their products effectively.

69
00:07:20,629 --> 00:07:27,110
And enterprises typically span many use cases.

70
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:37,430
A single enterprise customer may contain and use our software for many different product lines or for many different languages with very specific and niche applications.

71
00:07:37,430 --> 00:07:43,189
They may be serving internal customers or external customers.

72
00:07:43,189 --> 00:07:48,550
And this poses many operational and model development challenges that are really really interesting.

73
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:59,189
One of the key differences here is that in traditional data science testing, as Jacob alluded to earlier, we generally have a quite welldefined and narrowly scoped problem.

74
00:07:59,189 --> 00:08:03,830
And to answer some sort of question, for example, maybe we're trying to make predictions about some data.

75
00:08:03,830 --> 00:08:08,230
How well can we classify the domain of some audio given some features about that audio?

76
00:08:08,230 --> 00:08:12,309
But when it comes to enterprises,

77
00:08:12,309 --> 00:08:19,430
often times there's a much higher level of system complexity both within our system and the enterprise system

78
00:08:19,430 --> 00:08:28,390
that requires testing of both on the model side, which is the side that I'm more familiar with, and on the production side of serving that.

79
00:08:28,390 --> 00:08:36,630
And so when a customer encounters a problem, there's a decent amount of testing that first needs to happen on our side in order to determine did it happen somewhere on the request side?

80
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:40,790
Was it an issue with how the user submitted audio?

81
00:08:40,790 --> 00:08:42,550
Is it something specific about the audio itself?

82
00:08:42,550 --> 00:08:46,150
Did something go wrong in the production stack?

83
00:08:46,150 --> 00:08:48,230
Or is it actually a fundamental issue with the model?

84
00:08:48,230 --> 00:08:52,550
There's a lot of interdependencies between the model and the production code.

85
00:08:52,550 --> 00:08:58,710
There are also interdependencies between Deepgram Grams models and how they're used by the enterprise customer.

86
00:08:58,710 --> 00:09:02,630
We've also got a much much larger scale of data that we're talking about here.

87
00:09:02,630 --> 00:09:15,030
Traditional data science, we may be talking about tens or hundreds of thousands, millions of test examples, but our stack is serving tens of thousands of requests per second.

88
00:09:15,030 --> 00:09:21,430
And so this poses very unique data capturing, storage, filtering challenges in actually targeting the right type of data.

89
00:09:21,430 --> 00:09:28,870
And then we've also got the case that the edge cases in which they're used are innumerable.

90
00:09:28,870 --> 00:09:42,389
There are many many parameters to investigate across user API parameters that are specified to audio specific parameters that could be things about audio quality or acoustic conditions that are present.

91
00:09:42,389 --> 00:09:46,070
Could be down to inference parameters that we have set up on the model itself.

92
00:09:46,070 --> 00:09:50,389
This could be things like the duration of the audio.

93
00:09:50,389 --> 00:09:57,030
Are people submitting very very short segments of audio and expecting the same types of behavior as submitting minutelong clips?

94
00:09:57,030 --> 00:09:58,870
These are almost impossible to design out.

95
00:09:58,870 --> 00:10:04,470
And I'll let Jacob speak to some interesting cases that he's definitely run across on the edge case side.

96
00:10:04,480 --> 00:10:07,110
Yeah, we should get into some more stories here at some point, Connor.

97
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:08,710
That'll make for some fun listening.

98
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:16,710
Maybe briefly, Kchan, you asked a bit about how does this compare to software testing traditionally speaking.

99
00:10:16,710 --> 00:10:19,990
And one fun thing here is I have to say I only have a certain limited perspective.

100
00:10:19,990 --> 00:10:23,509
I haven't worked across the entire AI industry.

101
00:10:23,509 --> 00:10:36,710
I do have a useful comparison here because before Deepgram I worked in kind of traditional software consulting doing typical big enterprise application development things like payments processing and record storage and that sort of thing.

102
00:10:36,710 --> 00:10:46,710
I think that one of the big ideas that has emerged from software development very generally over the last few decades is we have a testing pyramid and of course many of your listeners would be familiar

103
00:10:46,710 --> 00:10:56,630
with this but you can walk through think about some application where there's a user interface and the user can update his birthday and there's probably going to be some unit testing

104
00:10:56,630 --> 00:11:02,710
that says hey if I pass in this object that represents a person's information it gets updated with the birthday okay we can ensure

105
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:15,350
that that part is correct and maybe you have an integ integration test that asserts that this object as you've modeled it in your code is correctly mapped into the domain of your database schema.

106
00:11:15,350 --> 00:11:22,550
Maybe you have something like a higher level integration test that asserts that all this works through a backend API.

107
00:11:22,550 --> 00:11:27,750
And then finally, like at the tippy top of your testing pyramid, the thing that's the slowest, most expensive to run,

108
00:11:27,750 --> 00:11:38,310
but really the most valuable for confirming that things work is you have some sort of endto-end test, maybe running through Selenium in a browser, and you're testing that this works through the user UI.

109
00:11:38,310 --> 00:11:39,910
So that's your traditional testing pyramid.

110
00:11:39,910 --> 00:11:43,750
And really, in a sense, this testing pyramid doesn't go away.

111
00:11:43,750 --> 00:11:51,990
We still need to have lots of small test cases that are the foundation, and we build up to some more expensive ones that operate near the top.

112
00:11:51,990 --> 00:12:04,790
It's just that what those layers look like have really, really, really changed because we're no longer testing the storage and retrieval of data in a data model, which is the vast majority of probably software applications that have ever been written.

113
00:12:04,790 --> 00:12:07,350
And now we have this compute intensive world.

114
00:12:07,350 --> 00:12:15,190
So at our lowest level, at this unit level, now we're thinking about operations in a neural network and their mathematical correctness.

115
00:12:15,190 --> 00:12:17,190
In one sense, this is still a foundational unit test.

116
00:12:17,190 --> 00:12:19,269
We can model this in functional code.

117
00:12:19,269 --> 00:12:24,389
In another sense, it's kind of tricky because now you're taking on this more intense mathematical domain.

118
00:12:24,389 --> 00:12:35,509
But then you move up a level in the stack and many of these lower level functions become a full models network or maybe there's even a pipeline of models whose inputs and outputs are wired up together.

119
00:12:35,509 --> 00:12:40,310
So now you want to test that that flow works.

120
00:12:40,310 --> 00:12:41,829
Probably in most AI systems you have a variety of features.

121
00:12:41,829 --> 00:12:47,830
And so now you're testing that that flow works with intersection of various parameters that users tune. Right?

122
00:12:47,830 --> 00:12:59,030
You see a lot of modern AI APIs where a lot of power is put in the hands of the user to configure things under the hood like the temperature used in different inference worlds.

123
00:12:59,030 --> 00:13:09,190
And so we're gradually working higher and higher up the stack and you eventually get into the world of okay, we can confirm that we get exactly the right outputs for exactly this one given input.

124
00:13:09,190 --> 00:13:13,670
And then you get into the data diversity explosion that Connor mentioned.

125
00:13:13,670 --> 00:13:20,790
the fact that in Deepgram's world, we're taking in arbitrary audio and audio can be encoded in a lot of different ways.

126
00:13:20,790 --> 00:13:22,470
You can say all sorts of things within it.

127
00:13:22,470 --> 00:13:24,949
And so now you're trying to deal with that space.

128
00:13:24,949 --> 00:13:31,190
And then finally, you get into I think this is the most powerful world for Deepgram and our customers to collaborate on,

129
00:13:31,190 --> 00:13:38,710
but it's the downstream integrations between say a foundational AI system and our customers systems.

130
00:13:38,710 --> 00:13:44,230
We've even seen cases where you can have AI outputs that improve.

131
00:13:44,230 --> 00:13:50,550
But if say Deepgram's speechtoext outputs are then used by a customer for some sort of NLP process

132
00:13:50,560 --> 00:13:59,910
and so now they're doing something like looking for product names mentioned or key topics discussed in internal meetings.

133
00:13:59,910 --> 00:14:07,269
Well, if your transcription output changes significantly in some way, then you might have made an improvement but thrown off a downstream model.

134
00:14:07,269 --> 00:14:13,590
And so you actually care about that very full endto-end flow and that it's the same testing pyramid but it's just cast in a new light.

135
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:18,389
Thanks for highlighting all those challenges.

136
00:14:18,389 --> 00:14:21,189
So who exactly is responsible for doing this testing?

137
00:14:21,189 --> 00:14:23,750
Is it the data scientist?

138
00:14:23,750 --> 00:14:26,310
Is it the ML engineer?

139
00:14:26,310 --> 00:14:29,511
I've heard this new term of an AI engineer. So who is it? Which role?

140
00:14:29,519 --> 00:14:34,629
I wish there was a single answer for you.

141
00:14:34,629 --> 00:14:41,590
Unfortunately or fortunately, the first part of this process is always to identify where exactly something is going wrong.

142
00:14:41,590 --> 00:14:51,351
And that comes with high levels of observability throughout the system, whether that's in the production system or whether that's within the model. Who owns it?

143
00:14:51,351 --> 00:14:59,269
Well, when we've identified where in the system things are going wrong, then that sort of dictates whether it's more on the ML engineer,

144
00:14:59,269 --> 00:15:04,069
let's say, or on the engineering side or on the research side.

145
00:15:04,069 --> 00:15:10,310
If it's a fundamental issue with the model where the model is producing hallucinations under some bad conditions,

146
00:15:10,310 --> 00:15:17,990
then that warrants a retrain and that warrants basically a revision to the model to ensure that that doesn't happen.

147
00:15:17,990 --> 00:15:26,310
We want to as much as possible make our models very robust to many different types of audio conditions and acoustic parameters.

148
00:15:26,310 --> 00:15:35,030
And so we want to make sure that we design that out as low as possible because that really helps the production side of things and making that far more streamline.

149
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:38,870
Yeah, I think these questions of roles are so fun, right?

150
00:15:38,870 --> 00:15:49,509
Like when the world is kind of shifting beneath our feet and all these new tools for developing technologies are coming out, I just I'm reminded of things like when the industrial revolution came about.

151
00:15:49,509 --> 00:15:58,470
All of a sudden you have like a factory floor manager and there's just no parallel for that for work out on a farm field, right?

152
00:15:58,470 --> 00:16:00,790
And you have this new profession that emerged.

153
00:16:00,790 --> 00:16:04,310
And so I think you're actually asking a pretty profound thing here really.

154
00:16:04,310 --> 00:16:07,990
The software engineers answer is we always want to just throw product managers under the bus, right?

155
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:12,069
So, it's whose responsibility is it's got to be product.

156
00:16:12,069 --> 00:16:13,590
I say that with a lot of love for our team.

157
00:16:13,590 --> 00:16:19,509
We can shout out Natalie and Sharon and Nevin and Peter and Nick because they do a great job for us here at Deepgram.

158
00:16:19,509 --> 00:16:24,870
But I think Connor kind of put the nail on the head here, which is that it has to be an interdisciplinary effort.

159
00:16:24,870 --> 00:16:35,110
There's a certain amount of rigid, low-level functional testing that a software engineer is going to absolutely knock out of the park when you ask them to look into this kind of area.

160
00:16:35,110 --> 00:16:44,790
And then there's thinking about a wide data domain and like even are you sampling correctly across the entire distribution of data

161
00:16:44,790 --> 00:16:51,189
that represents your production environment and what kinds of potential biases might you be encountering in trying to construct your test set?

162
00:16:51,189 --> 00:16:58,389
like those are questions that are really well answered by somebody like Connor with a PhD that I very clearly lack.

163
00:16:58,389 --> 00:17:08,390
And so I think we're just seeing that the age-old story quality is always an interdisiplinary problem and we're just finding new ways to weave together the right disciplines to address the quality issue.

164
00:17:08,400 --> 00:17:10,310
Yeah. Yeah.

165
00:17:10,310 --> 00:17:16,069
And you mentioned the AI engineer role and while we don't have an explicit AI engineer role at Deepgram,

166
00:17:16,079 --> 00:17:28,309
really what I think about and everybody's got their own preconceived notions about what an AI engineer does, but it's really somebody who can sort of understand I think both sides of the coin,

167
00:17:28,309 --> 00:17:35,430
understand both the production side, the hosting of models, but also the training and model development side of the house.

168
00:17:35,430 --> 00:17:38,150
And you may not need to be sort of an expert in both,

169
00:17:38,150 --> 00:17:45,430
but the ability to share a common vernacular with say folks who are more on the production side

170
00:17:45,430 --> 00:17:57,590
and perhaps folks who are more on the research side, being able to have that shared understanding and especially when developing the model, being able to understand the implications of design decisions that you're making there.

171
00:17:57,590 --> 00:18:07,430
For example, if you made a model that was 70 billion parameters, from an accuracy perspective, however you want to measure that, it will likely do very well.

172
00:18:07,440 --> 00:18:14,230
However, from a latency perspective, you're going to take a massive hit there without significant amount of compute.

173
00:18:14,240 --> 00:18:26,150
So being able to understand those types of interdependencies and where trade-offs are worth making is I think where the quote unquote role of AI engineer is going

174
00:18:26,150 --> 00:18:31,190
and is going to be critical in handling these very interdisciplinary projects and types of problems.

175
00:18:31,200 --> 00:18:34,391
Thank you. That helps.

176
00:18:34,391 --> 00:18:39,270
So how does LLM make this already difficult problem even worse?

177
00:18:39,280 --> 00:18:45,350
Well LLMs, we use LLM a fair bit here at Deepgram.

178
00:18:45,350 --> 00:18:47,430
We've got a new feature, a new product line.

179
00:18:47,430 --> 00:18:50,470
That's our voice agent API.

180
00:18:50,470 --> 00:18:56,230
And really what that is is we have the ability for users to bring their own LLM

181
00:18:56,230 --> 00:19:07,029
to a voice agent party where you use Deepgrams speechtoext on the front end and Deepgram Grams texttospech on the back end with your own LLM in the middle.

182
00:19:07,029 --> 00:19:13,990
LLMs are notorious for hallucinations and a whole host of other problems if they aren't formulated correctly.

183
00:19:13,990 --> 00:19:22,870
But it makes evaluating the quality of LLMs quite a challenge and especially in the context of this voice agent API.

184
00:19:22,870 --> 00:19:29,430
Our models expect a certain type of input and a certain type of output and so stringing them together always presents unique challenges.

185
00:19:29,430 --> 00:19:40,150
For example, on the texttospech side of the house, often times there are ways that things are said that when they're written down, they become nebulous.

186
00:19:40,150 --> 00:19:50,230
For example, if you typed in or if you were to write 14 km, it could be that the person said 14 km.

187
00:19:50,230 --> 00:19:54,630
It could be that the person said 14 kilome.

188
00:19:54,630 --> 00:19:57,830
It could be 14 km.

189
00:19:57,830 --> 00:20:00,310
it could be 14,000 m meters, right?

190
